## Executive Summary

This assignment extended SecureText with end-to-end encryption and time-based session management. Messages are now protected using an ephemeral, per-session ECDH key exchange on the P-256 curve (a two-ephemeral, zero-static pairwise key establishment scheme) [5], with HKDF-SHA256 deriving a 256-bit AES key from each pair's shared secret [3]. That key encrypts message content with AES-256-GCM before it ever reaches the server, so the server relays only ciphertext and public keys and has no way to recover message content, satisfying confidentiality and giving GCM's authentication tag built-in tamper detection on top of the existing HMAC layer.

Session management was extended with an automatic 30-minute inactivity timeout, a proactive 5-minute warning pushed to idle clients, forced re-authentication on expiry, and cleanup of session-specific key material on both timeout and manual logout. Because a new ECDH key pair is generated on every login and discarded on session end, compromise of one session's derived key does not expose messages from a different session, providing forward secrecy at the session level. The implementation was validated through direct testing between two users: matching independently-derived shared keys, successful encryption/decryption with correct MAC verification, confirmed nonce uniqueness across identical plaintext, tamper detection on corrupted ciphertext, and a standalone script confirming an old session's key cannot decrypt a new session's messages.

