### 2.2 Task 8: Authenticated Encryption (AES-256-GCM end-to-end; the server only routes ciphertext)

#### 2.2.1 Objective

Ensure messages sent between users are confidential and tamper-evident end-to-end, using the shared key derived in Task 7, such that the server only ever handles ciphertext and cannot read message content even if its database or memory were inspected.

#### 2.2.2 Implementation Details

Message encryption uses AES-256-GCM via the cryptography library's AESGCM class, keyed with the 32-byte session key from get_shared_key. In send_message, the client generates a fresh random 12-byte nonce with os.urandom(12) for every message, encrypts the plaintext, and concatenates the nonce and ciphertext (which includes GCM's authentication tag) into a single hex string sent to the server as the message content. The existing HMAC (compute_mac) is computed over this encrypted content rather than the plaintext, so message integrity checking still applies to what's actually transmitted. On the receiving side, listen_for_messages derives the same shared key with the sender, splits the received hex back into nonce and ciphertext, and calls AESGCM(shared_key).decrypt to recover the plaintext. No server-side changes were needed for encryption itself the server already only relayed whatever string was in content; it just now relays ciphertext instead of plaintext.

#### 2.2.3 Challenges and Solutions

Since a fresh nonce is generated per message, sending the same plaintext twice produces different ciphertext each time this was confirmed by temporarily printing the encrypted content value for two identical messages and comparing the hex output. Decryption failures (e.g. from a tampered or corrupted ciphertext) are caught and surfaced to the user as "DECRYPTION FAILED" rather than crashing the client.

#### 2.2.4 Testing and Validation

1. Alice sends Bob a message; Bob's console shows the correctly decrypted plaintext with a VALID MAC status.
2. The same plaintext message sent twice produces two different ciphertext strings (nonce uniqueness).
3. The server's own view of the message (content field) is inspected and shown to be an undecipherable hex blob, confirming the server cannot read message content.
4. A manually corrupted ciphertext fails to decrypt, demonstrating GCM's built-in tamper detection.

