### 2.3 Task 9:  Session Management (30-minute expiry, secure key cleanup, forward secrecy)

#### 2.3.1 Objective

Automatically expire a user's session after a period of inactivity, warn the user before that happens, force re-authentication once expired, and securely clear session-specific cryptographic material (the ECDH private key and derived shared keys) so it cannot be reused or recovered after expiry or logout.

#### 2.3.2 Implementation Details

The server tracks a last-activity timestamp per logged-in user in self.last_activity. A background thread (session_monitor), started alongside the main server loop, checks every 5 seconds whether any active user has crossed the warning threshold or the full timeout, and pushes a message directly over that user's existing socket connection without waiting for the client to send a command. This is what allows the warning and expiry to appear on their own while a user is idle, rather than only being visible the next time they happen to interact with the menu. On expiry, the server also removes the user's entry from active_connections, session_type, session_action_count, last_activity, and public_keys, and clears them from warned_users. On the client side, receiving a session-expired push clears self.logged_in, self.ecdh_private_key, and self.session_keys, so the next login is forced to generate an entirely new ECDH key pair rather than reusing the old one. Manual logout (menu option 3) was updated to perform the same cleanup on both sides, so a user does not need to wait for a timeout for their keys to be cleared.

#### 2.3.3 Challenges and Solutions

The first implementation checked session status and calculated the warning only when the client explicitly requested it (a "Session Status" menu option), which meant the warning would never appear unless the user happened to check at the right moment, and in one case produced two contradictory remaining-time values in a single response because the activity timestamp was being reset by the status check itself before the warning was calculated from it. This was resolved by moving to the background thread approach described above, so warnings and expiry are pushed proactively and are not dependent on the client polling, and by making sure the status check itself does not count as activity that resets the timer.

#### 2.3.4 Testing and Validation

1. Checking session status immediately after login shows nearly the full remaining time.
2. Leaving a session idle without any interaction results in an unprompted warning message appearing at the configured warning threshold.
3. Continuing to leave the session idle past the full timeout results in an unprompted expiration message, after which any further action forces the user back to the login menu.
4. Logging back in after expiry generates a new ECDH key pair, confirmed by comparing the full derived shared key with a peer before and after expiry.
5. Logging out manually and logging back in also produces a new derived shared key, confirming cleanup is not limited to timeout-based expiry.
6. A shared key captured from an earlier session cannot decrypt ciphertext from a later session (checked with a standalone script), demonstrating forward secrecy from the key rotation described above.

