## 5. Performance Evaluation
Rather than micro-benchmarking individual cryptographic calls in isolation, this section describes where E2EE introduces cost into the existing message flow and reasons about its impact qualitatively.

**Analysis**:
E2EE adds cost at two points in the message lifecycle. First, key establishment: the first time two users message each other in a session, one extra round trip to the server occurs to fetch the peer's public key (GET_KEY), followed by a local ECDH computation and an HKDF derivation. This only happens once per peer per session, since the result is cached in session_keys; every subsequent message between the same two users in that session reuses the cached key and pays no further key-exchange cost. Second, per-message cost: every send now includes generating a random nonce and running the message through AES-256-GCM before it reaches the existing MAC and socket-send logic, and every receive reverses that with a GCM decrypt before the message is displayed.

Both of these operations are standard, well-optimized primitives (P-256 ECDH and AES-GCM are both hardware-accelerated on most modern CPUs) operating on small inputs, short text messages and single elliptic-curve points, so in practice they add negligible, sub-millisecond overhead compared to the cost that already dominates this application: the network round trip through the TCP socket and the existing PBKDF2/TOTP-based authentication flow. The one-time per-peer key exchange cost is the only part of this that could be noticeable at all, and only on the very first message exchanged with a new peer in a session.

**Limitations**
this analysis is qualitative rather than measured; if quantitative numbers are required, a standalone timing script exercising these same primitives independently of the client/server would be the way to get them without instrumenting the application itself.

